From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Net4x.Gtk.Drawing defects found by functional tests

Behavioural defects surfaced by the Net4x.Gtk.Drawing.Tests functional suite:

Bitmap/Image
 - the pixel buffer was never allocated, so a new Bitmap had no pixels
 - GetPixel/SetPixel addressed the wrong bytes and never invalidated the cache
 - scaling, cloning, MakeTransparent, LockBits/UnlockBits and RotateFlip were stubs
 - Save(Stream, ImageFormat) ignored the requested format
 - HorizontalResolution/VerticalResolution, PhysicalDimension and GetBounds were hard-coded

Graphics
 - Dispose did not flush, so drawing could be lost
 - SetClip(Region) was missing; SetClip(GraphicsPath), ExcludeClip and IntersectClip(Region) were no-ops
 - TranslateClip(int, int) called itself and overflowed the stack
 - ResetTransform only undid rotation; MultiplyTransform mutated a throwaway copy
 - the Transform property threw on get and ignored set
 - ConvertToMatrix applied the default zero scale (collapsing every matrix), passed degrees to Cairo as
   radians, and passed null to Cairo.Matrix.Multiply (a native abort)
 - clip bounds, DPI, TextRenderingHint, containers, TransformPoints, MeasureCharacterRanges,
   image blitting and curve drawing were stubs or wrong

GraphicsPath / Matrix / Region / Font / Icon
 - Reset did not clear the path; Transform ignored the stored primitives
 - IsOutlineVisible reported the filled interior as being on the outline
 - Matrix.TransformPoints/TransformVectors did not apply the transform; Translate did not move it;
   OffsetX/OffsetY did not track dx/dy
 - Region.Translate did not move the region, IsEmpty called an infinite region empty, and RegionData
   carried nothing so a Region could not be round-tripped
 - Font.Height/GetHeight and Icon.Size were stubs

Emulation gaps from EMULATION-GAPS.md
 - the eight Graphics.IsVisible overloads all threw NotImplementedException; they now answer from the
   clip bounds the class already tracks (SS4.1)
 - SmoothingMode, InterpolationMode and CompositingMode were auto-properties with no consumer anywhere in
   the file, so setting them read back correctly and changed nothing; they now map onto Cairo's Antialias
   and Operator and Gdk's interpolation filter (SS2.8)
 - PageUnit and PageScale were inert, so a Graphics set to Millimeter or Inch still drew at pixel
   coordinates - a geometric error; they now fold into the current transform (SS2.8)
 - Font.FromHfont / FromHdc / FromLogFont returned Arial 12 for any input, a silent wrong font; they now
   throw PlatformNotSupportedException, which an integrator can act on (SS2.11)

 - MeasureString measured a single line and then clamped to the layout area, so text that wraps in
   WinForms reported the width of the box and the height of one line; it now lays the words out against
   the available width and reports the real number of lines
 - added the missing SystemBrushes and SystemFonts types, which SystemColors/SystemPens already implied

 - TextureBrush was the one core GDI+ brush type missing (SolidBrush, HatchBrush, LinearGradientBrush and
   PathGradientBrush were all present); it is backed by a Cairo surface pattern with tile/clamp wrap
 - SystemIcons was missing; the icons come from the GTK icon theme where it has a match and are drawn
   programmatically otherwise, so no icon assets need shipping
 - Matrix.Elements, IsIdentity and IsInvertible threw, which blocked inspecting or serialising a transform
   even though the transform operations themselves worked (SS4.4)

Region is no longer a single rectangle (SS2.7)
 - the interior was one RectangleF, so Union of two disjoint rectangles reported the gap between them as
   inside, Exclude shrank the bounding box instead of leaving a hole, and Xor of two partly overlapping
   rectangles returned their *union* - keeping the overlap an exclusive-or must remove
 - the interior is now a list of disjoint rectangles, the band list GDI+ itself uses, making the whole
   combine family exact for rectangular inputs and making non-rectangular regions representable
 - region-vs-region combines went through the other region's GraphicsPath bounds; they now work on the
   band lists directly
 - GetRegionScans returned the single bounding rectangle; it now returns the actual bands
 - IsVisible tested the bounding rectangle, so a point in the hole of an L-shaped region counted as inside
 - Region.Clone shared the interior with the original
 - Graphics.SetClip(Region) reduced the region to its bounding rectangle, so clipping to a non-rectangular
   region silently clipped to its bounds; it now clips to the real bands

 - ToolboxBitmapAttribute was missing. Because it decorates a class declaration, an entire custom-control
   library failed to compile rather than one call site (SS3.7)

 - the Cairo surface and context behind Graphics.FromImage were STATIC, so every Graphics created that way
   shared one surface: allocating a second disposed the first one's surface out from under it and two
   Graphics objects over two different bitmaps could not coexist. They are now per-instance, and a Graphics
   only disposes a context it owns. This is the root cause of the "CreateGraphics disposes the previous
   surface" behaviour noted in SS2.5
 - added BufferedGraphics, BufferedGraphicsContext and BufferedGraphicsManager, the standard flicker-free
   painting recipe, which the static surface had made impossible (SS3.7)
 - the Graphics interop entry points (FromHdc x3, FromHwnd x2, GetHdc, GetHalftonePalette) threw
   NotImplementedException; they now throw PlatformNotSupportedException as SSP4 recommends
 - TextContrast threw from its getter while its setter silently accepted, so a value could be assigned and
   never read back

 - Pen.PenType threw; it now derives the pen type from the kind of brush the pen strokes with (SS4.4)
 - Icon.FromHandle threw NotImplementedException; it now throws PlatformNotSupportedException naming the
   alternative, like the other GDI-handle entry points (SS4.4)
 - CustomLineCap discarded its constructor arguments, threw from every getter while its setters silently
   accepted, and Clone returned null; it now stores, round-trips and clones its state. A custom cap still
   does not render - Cairo has no user-defined cap primitive - which stays on the gap list (SS4.4)

---
diff --git a/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/Bitmap.cs b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/Bitmap.cs
index b6ec9d3a..d4cbf94a 100644
--- a/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/Bitmap.cs
+++ b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/Bitmap.cs
@@ -233,6 +233,16 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
         this.Width = width;
         this.Height = height;
         this.PixelFormat = format;
+
+        // Allocate a real, writable pixel buffer (fully transparent) so GetPixel/SetPixel and Save operate
+        // on actual pixels rather than an empty image. Invalid (non-positive) dimensions are left without a
+        // pixbuf so that consumers such as Graphics.FromImage still reject them.
+        if (width > 0 && height > 0)
+        {
+            var pixbuf = new Gdk.Pixbuf(Gdk.Colorspace.Rgb, true, 8, width, height);
+            pixbuf.Fill(0x00000000u);
+            this.Pixbuf = pixbuf;
+        }
     }
 
     /// <summary>
@@ -294,7 +304,16 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     public Bitmap(Image original, int width, int height)
         : this(width, height, PixelFormat.Format32bppArgb)
     {
-        this.PixbufData = original.PixbufData;
+        // Actually scale the source to the requested size rather than copying its raw data unchanged.
+        var source = original.Pixbuf;
+        if (source != null)
+        {
+            this.Pixbuf = source.ScaleSimple(Math.Max(1, width), Math.Max(1, height), Gdk.InterpType.Bilinear);
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            this.PixbufData = original.PixbufData;
+        }
     }
 
     internal Bitmap(byte[]? pixbuf) : base(pixbuf)
@@ -384,8 +403,29 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public Bitmap Clone(Rectangle rect, PixelFormat format)
     {
+        // Previously this copied the WHOLE source buffer into the clone, which also reset the clone's
+        // size back to the source's - so the requested sub-rectangle was ignored entirely.
+        var clone = new Bitmap(rect.Width, rect.Height, format);
+        var source = this.Pixbuf;
+        var target = clone.Pixbuf;
+
+        if (source == null || target == null || rect.Width <= 0 || rect.Height <= 0)
+        {
+            clone.PixbufData = (byte[]?)this.PixbufData?.Clone();
+            return clone;
+        }
 
-        return new Bitmap(rect.Width, rect.Height, format) { PixbufData = (byte[]?)this.PixbufData?.Clone() };
+        var x = Math.Max(0, Math.Min(rect.X, source.Width));
+        var y = Math.Max(0, Math.Min(rect.Y, source.Height));
+        var width = Math.Min(rect.Width, source.Width - x);
+        var height = Math.Min(rect.Height, source.Height - y);
+        if (width > 0 && height > 0)
+        {
+            source.CopyArea(x, y, width, height, target, 0, 0);
+            clone.InvalidatePixbufDataCache();
+        }
+
+        return clone;
     }
 
     /// <summary>
@@ -502,8 +542,25 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public Color GetPixel(int x, int y)
     {
+        var pixbuf = this.Pixbuf;
+        if (pixbuf == null)
+        {
+            return Color.Empty;
+        }
 
-        return Color.FromArgb(x * y);
+        if (x < 0 || y < 0 || x >= pixbuf.Width || y >= pixbuf.Height)
+        {
+            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(x < 0 || x >= pixbuf.Width ? nameof(x) : nameof(y));
+        }
+
+        var channels = pixbuf.NChannels;
+        var offset = (y * pixbuf.Rowstride) + (x * channels);
+        var pixels = pixbuf.Pixels;
+        byte r = Marshal.ReadByte(pixels, offset);
+        byte g = Marshal.ReadByte(pixels, offset + 1);
+        byte b = Marshal.ReadByte(pixels, offset + 2);
+        byte a = channels >= 4 ? Marshal.ReadByte(pixels, offset + 3) : (byte)255;
+        return Color.FromArgb(a, r, g, b);
     }
 
     /// <summary>
@@ -581,7 +638,26 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public BitmapData LockBits(Rectangle rect, ImageLockMode flags, PixelFormat format, BitmapData bitmapData)
     {
+        // Was returning an unpopulated BitmapData (all zeroes). Describe the real pixel buffer so callers
+        // can read/write it, pointing Scan0 at the requested rectangle's first pixel.
+        bitmapData ??= new BitmapData();
+
+        var pixbuf = this.Pixbuf;
+        if (pixbuf == null)
+        {
+            return bitmapData;
+        }
+
+        var x = Math.Max(0, Math.Min(rect.X, pixbuf.Width));
+        var y = Math.Max(0, Math.Min(rect.Y, pixbuf.Height));
+        var width = rect.Width <= 0 ? pixbuf.Width - x : Math.Min(rect.Width, pixbuf.Width - x);
+        var height = rect.Height <= 0 ? pixbuf.Height - y : Math.Min(rect.Height, pixbuf.Height - y);
 
+        bitmapData.Width = Math.Max(0, width);
+        bitmapData.Height = Math.Max(0, height);
+        bitmapData.Stride = pixbuf.Rowstride;
+        bitmapData.PixelFormat = format;
+        bitmapData.Scan0 = IntPtr.Add(pixbuf.Pixels, (y * pixbuf.Rowstride) + (x * pixbuf.NChannels));
         return bitmapData;
     }
 
@@ -626,7 +702,24 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public void MakeTransparent(Color transparentColor)
     {
+        // Was a no-op, so the requested colour never became transparent.
+        var pixbuf = this.Pixbuf;
+        if (pixbuf == null)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
 
+        for (var y = 0; y < pixbuf.Height; y++)
+        {
+            for (var x = 0; x < pixbuf.Width; x++)
+            {
+                var c = GetPixel(x, y);
+                if (c.R == transparentColor.R && c.G == transparentColor.G && c.B == transparentColor.B)
+                {
+                    SetPixel(x, y, Color.FromArgb(0, c.R, c.G, c.B));
+                }
+            }
+        }
     }
 
     /// <summary>
@@ -648,7 +741,31 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public void SetPixel(int x, int y, Color color)
     {
+        var pixbuf = this.Pixbuf;
+        if (pixbuf == null)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
 
+        if (x < 0 || y < 0 || x >= pixbuf.Width || y >= pixbuf.Height)
+        {
+            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(x < 0 || x >= pixbuf.Width ? nameof(x) : nameof(y));
+        }
+
+        var channels = pixbuf.NChannels;
+        var offset = (y * pixbuf.Rowstride) + (x * channels);
+        var pixels = pixbuf.Pixels;
+        Marshal.WriteByte(pixels, offset, color.R);
+        Marshal.WriteByte(pixels, offset + 1, color.G);
+        Marshal.WriteByte(pixels, offset + 2, color.B);
+        if (channels >= 4)
+        {
+            Marshal.WriteByte(pixels, offset + 3, color.A);
+        }
+
+        // Pixels were mutated in place; drop only the cached encoding (not the live pixbuf) so
+        // PixbufData/Save re-encode from the current pixels.
+        InvalidatePixbufDataCache();
     }
 
     /// <summary>
@@ -668,7 +785,8 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public void SetResolution(float xDpi, float yDpi)
     {
-
+        // Was empty, so HorizontalResolution/VerticalResolution never reflected the requested DPI.
+        SetResolutionCore(xDpi, yDpi);
     }
 
     /// <summary>
@@ -684,7 +802,8 @@ public class Bitmap : Image
     /// </exception>
     public void UnlockBits(BitmapData bitmapdata)
     {
-
+        // The caller may have written through Scan0, so drop the cached encoding.
+        InvalidatePixbufDataCache();
     }
 
     private static Stream GetResourceStream(Type type, string resource)
diff --git a/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphics.cs b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphics.cs
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..1002a951
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphics.cs
@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+namespace System.Drawing;
+
+/// <summary>
+/// Provides a graphics buffer for double buffering.
+/// </summary>
+/// <remarks>
+/// The buffered-graphics family was missing entirely, so the standard flicker-free custom-painting
+/// recipe — allocate a buffer, draw into it, render it in one go — did not compile. The buffer here is an
+/// off-screen <see cref="T:System.Drawing.Bitmap" />; <see cref="M:System.Drawing.BufferedGraphics.Render" />
+/// blits it onto the target surface.
+/// </remarks>
+public sealed class BufferedGraphics : IDisposable
+{
+    private readonly Rectangle _targetRectangle;
+
+    private Bitmap? _buffer;
+
+    private Graphics? _bufferGraphics;
+
+    private Graphics? _targetGraphics;
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Initializes a new buffer of the given size drawing onto the given target.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <param name="targetGraphics">The surface the buffer is rendered onto, if any.</param>
+    /// <param name="targetRectangle">The area the buffer covers.</param>
+    internal BufferedGraphics(Graphics? targetGraphics, Rectangle targetRectangle)
+    {
+        _targetGraphics = targetGraphics;
+        _targetRectangle = targetRectangle;
+        _buffer = new Bitmap(Math.Max(1, targetRectangle.Width), Math.Max(1, targetRectangle.Height));
+        _bufferGraphics = Graphics.FromImage(_buffer);
+    }
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Gets the surface to draw the buffered content onto.
+    /// </summary>
+    public Graphics Graphics => _bufferGraphics
+                                ?? throw new ObjectDisposedException(nameof(BufferedGraphics));
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Writes the buffer onto the surface it was allocated against.
+    /// </summary>
+    public void Render() => Render(_targetGraphics);
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Writes the buffer onto the given surface.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <param name="target">The surface to write onto. Nothing happens when it is null.</param>
+    public void Render(Graphics? target)
+    {
+        if (target == null || _buffer == null || _bufferGraphics == null)
+        {
+            return;
+        }
+
+        // Flush the buffer's own drawing before blitting it, otherwise the last operations are still
+        // sitting in the Cairo context.
+        _bufferGraphics.Flush();
+        target.DrawImage(_buffer, _targetRectangle.X, _targetRectangle.Y);
+    }
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Writes the buffer onto the given device context. Not available on this platform.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <param name="targetDC">A handle to a device context.</param>
+    public void Render(IntPtr targetDC) =>
+        throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
+            "GDI device contexts are not available on this platform; render onto a Graphics instead.");
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Releases the buffer.
+    /// </summary>
+    public void Dispose()
+    {
+        _bufferGraphics?.Dispose();
+        _bufferGraphics = null;
+        _buffer?.Dispose();
+        _buffer = null;
+        _targetGraphics = null;
+    }
+}
diff --git a/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphicsContext.cs b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphicsContext.cs
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..440ada6d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphicsContext.cs
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+namespace System.Drawing;
+
+/// <summary>
+/// Provides methods for creating graphics buffers that can be used for double buffering.
+/// </summary>
+/// <remarks>
+/// Part of the buffered-graphics family, which was missing entirely.
+/// </remarks>
+public sealed class BufferedGraphicsContext : IDisposable
+{
+    private Size _maximumBuffer = new(225, 96);
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Gets or sets the maximum size of a buffer to keep in memory.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <exception cref="T:System.ArgumentException">
+    /// The width or height is not positive.
+    /// </exception>
+    public Size MaximumBuffer
+    {
+        get => _maximumBuffer;
+        set
+        {
+            if (value.Width <= 0 || value.Height <= 0)
+            {
+                throw new ArgumentException("The maximum buffer size must be positive.", nameof(value));
+            }
+
+            _maximumBuffer = value;
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Creates a buffer of the given size drawing onto the given surface.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <param name="targetGraphics">The surface the buffer will be rendered onto.</param>
+    /// <param name="targetRectangle">The area the buffer covers.</param>
+    /// <returns>The allocated buffer.</returns>
+    public BufferedGraphics Allocate(Graphics? targetGraphics, Rectangle targetRectangle) =>
+        new(targetGraphics, targetRectangle);
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Creates a buffer of the given size for the given device context. Not available on this platform.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <param name="targetDC">A handle to a device context.</param>
+    /// <param name="targetRectangle">The area the buffer covers.</param>
+    public BufferedGraphics Allocate(IntPtr targetDC, Rectangle targetRectangle) =>
+        throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
+            "GDI device contexts are not available on this platform; allocate against a Graphics instead.");
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Releases any cached buffer this context is holding.
+    /// </summary>
+    public void Invalidate()
+    {
+    }
+
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Releases the resources used by this context.
+    /// </summary>
+    public void Dispose()
+    {
+    }
+}
diff --git a/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphicsManager.cs b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphicsManager.cs
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..ef56f3f6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Net4x.Gtk.Drawing/System/Drawing/BufferedGraphicsManager.cs
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+namespace System.Drawing;
+
+/// <summary>
+/// Provides access to the default <see cref="T:System.Drawing.BufferedGraphicsContext" /> for the
+/// application domain.
+/// </summary>
+/// <remarks>
+/// Part of the buffered-graphics family, which was missing entirely. This is the entry point of the
+/// standard flicker-free painting recipe:
+/// <code>
+/// using var buffer = BufferedGraphicsManager.Current.Allocate(e.Graphics, ClientRectangle);
+/// buffer.Graphics.Clear(BackColor);
+/// // ... draw ...
+/// buffer.Render();
+/// </code>
+/// </remarks>
+public static class BufferedGraphicsManager
+{
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Gets the default context for the current application domain.
+    /// </summary>
+    public static BufferedGraphicsContext Current { get; } = new();
+}
